WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:05.280
What are more common in high dimensional&nbsp;
spaces? Local minima or saddle points?

00:00:05.280 --> 00:00:07.600
It’s tempting to think it is the local minima,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:07.600 --> 00:00:11.920
because they are often problematic in&nbsp;
machine learning (at least in theory).&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:11.920 --> 00:00:16.240
However, the right answer to this AI&nbsp;
Coffee Break Quiz is “saddle points”.

00:00:16.240 --> 00:00:21.440
In a nutshell: To tell local minima from saddle&nbsp;
points apart, we look at the Hessian matrix,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.440 --> 00:00:26.000
which is sort-of the second derivative&nbsp;
of a function, like the loss function,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.000 --> 00:00:29.840
that takes as input a vector and outputs a scalar.

00:00:29.840 --> 00:00:34.000
For a local minimum, the Hessian&nbsp;
has only positive eigenvalues,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:34.000 --> 00:00:38.800
for a saddle point it has a mixture&nbsp;
of positive and negative eigenvalues.

00:00:38.800 --> 00:00:43.760
Now think about it: what is more likely&nbsp;
to happen? To have exclusively n positive&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:43.760 --> 00:00:49.360
eigenvalues in an n-dimensional space? Or a&nbsp;
mixture of both negative and positive signs?

00:00:49.360 --> 00:00:53.280
Well, because a mix is more likely,&nbsp;
saddle points are more likely to occur.

00:00:53.280 --> 00:00:58.800
They come with small gradients in their vicinity,&nbsp;
making gradient-based optimization harder.

